   "theory": "Follow on\n\nIn a test match, on completion of the first innings, if some team obtains a lead of 200 runs over the other team, it can ask the other team to play again before its own turn.\n\nDeclaration of the close of an innings\n\nThe captain of the batting team can announce the end of his innings at any time. In this case there will be an interval of 10 minutes between the two innings. The umpires and the captain of the opposing team will be informed of this decision some time beforehand, so that seven minutes may be available for rolling the pitch.\n\nStart, close and interval of play\n\nAt the start of every innings and after every day’s play and intervals, when play begins, the umpire standing at the bowling end will call “Play”. After this, if some team refuses to play, it will lose the match. After the umpire calls “Play”, the ball will not be thrown to any player for practice.\n\nThe umpire, before every fixed interval, at the end of every day’s play and at the end of the match, will call the word “Time” and will lift the bails from the wickets. The umpires will permit such intervals as have been mutually decided. Between two innings 10 minutes and for the coming of a new player not more than 2 minutes will be given.\n\nScore\n\nThe score is made by running between the wickets on both sides, and by wide ball, no ball, four and six. The batsman, after hitting the ball, runs towards the wickets at the other end. On the other side his partner runs towards his wicket. Before the ball comes, as many times as they run, that many runs are added to the score. If the ball played by the batsman, touching the ground, crosses the boundary, the umpire will signal a four, and 4 …",
